reates its own internal thundering herd during recovery."],"mechanism":"In a decentralized system, the lack of centralized admission control means that when one small service fails, its load shifts to others without a global coordinator to throttle requests. This leads to a cascading timeout cycle (SRC_006) where the network's 'independence' actually facilitates the spread of failure through uncoordinated retry loops.","optional_refs":["SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_006"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"scratch_proposal":{"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Could we define a 'Correlation Coefficient of Infrastructure' (CCI)? If we map every service to its dependency tree (OS, Kernel, Libs, Cloud Provider, Power Grid), the resilience of a decentralized network is inversely proportional to the overlap of these trees. If the overlap > 0 for a critical path, the network is effectively centralized regarding that failure mode.","why_keep_this":"Provides a quantitative path toward answering the 'evidence' part of the problem."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Is there a point where 'too much' redundancy in a centralized system actually increases the probability of correlated failure due to the complexity of the management layer?","related_refs":["SRC_005"]}]}}